   689	First, we will prove a numerical upper bound for $c$ in an irregular $D(4)$-quadruple $\{a,b,c,d\}$. The proof follows \cite[Proposition 4]{duje_kon} and \cite[Lemma 13]{fil_xy4}. In the next lemma $(v_m)$ and $(w_n)$ denote sequences of solutions described as in (\ref{niz vm}) and (\ref{niz wm}) but with $a$ instead of $a_2$, i.e. as in \cite{mbt}. 
   690	\begin{lemma}\label{lema: nume_c}
   691	    Let $\{a,b,c,d\}$ be a $D(4)$-quadruple such that $a<b<c<d_+<d$,  $c>b^{2.8}$, $b>\max\{4a,10^5\}$. Then $c<10^{2157}$ and $m<3.65\cdot 10^{21}$.
